   "spoken": "If White answers c5 with d5, don't grab with Nxd5 — White counter-sacrifices with the desperado Nxf7 and holds. Play e6 instead, challenging the d5-pawn, and White, undeveloped, is simply lost.",
   "kind": "branch",
   "teaches": "Meet a central pawn wedge by undermining it with a pawn, not by grabbing it into a desperado.",
   "reanchor": true
  },
  {
   "ply": 19,
   "t": 1231.5,
   "fen": "rnbq1rk1/pp2ppbp/6p1/2PnN3/2P5/2N5/PP3PPP/R1BQKB1R b KQ - 0 10",
   "line": [
    "Nd5",
    "dxc5"
   ],
   "spoken": "Deep in these lines the key resource is a quiet e5, pinning whichever piece recaptured and winning material — and two pieces for a rook, so often misjudged, favors the side with the two pieces in almost every middlegame.",
   "kind": "branch",
   "teaches": "Two minor pieces usually outweigh a rook in the middlegame; don't fear giving up the exchange for them.",
   "reanchor": true
  },
